    title: 'Matrix operations and determinants',
    body: 'A matrix is a rectangle of numbers arranged in rows and columns, and its determinant is one number worked out from those entries. UTME asks about matrices regularly because finding an inverse, testing a system and solving equations all rest on them.\n\nHere\'s the idea: some matrix operations behave like ordinary arithmetic and some do not. Adding and subtracting works entry by entry, but only when the two matrices have the same order. Multiplication is never entry by entry.\n\nStep 1 - Take the first row of the left matrix against the first column of the right matrix.\nStep 2 - Multiply matching entries and add them; that sum becomes one entry of the answer.\nStep 3 - Check the orders: a 2 x 3 matrix times a 3 x 4 matrix gives a 2 x 4 matrix, because the inner numbers must match.\n\nScalar multiplication scales every entry: 2 x [[1, 3], [4, 5]] = [[2, 6], [8, 10]]. The transpose turns rows into columns, and a matrix always has the same determinant as its transpose. For a 2 by 2 matrix [[a, b], [c, d]] the determinant is ad - bc, so [[2, 3], [1, 4]] gives (2 x 4) - (3 x 1) = 5. A 3 by 3 determinant is expanded along the row holding the most zeros, using the sign pattern + - +; [[2, 1, 3], [1, 0, 2], [4, 5, 1]] works out to 2.\n\nExample: Find the inverse of A = [[3, 5], [2, 3]].\nStep 1 - Determinant: ad - bc = (3 x 3) - (5 x 2) = 9 - 10 = -1.\nStep 2 - Formula: inverse = 1/(ad - bc) x [[d, -b], [-c, a]] = 1/(-1) x [[3, -5], [-2, 3]].\nStep 3 - Multiply the fraction through: [[-3, 5], [2, -3]].\nStep 4 - Check by multiplying back: the top left is (3 x -3) + (5 x 2) = -9 + 10 = 1, the top right is (3 x 5) + (5 x -3) = 0, and the bottom left is (2 x -3) + (3 x 2) = 0, so the product is the identity matrix and the inverse is confirmed.\n\nAlso: Try the multiplication, which is the operation most candidates get wrong. The question reads, "The product of [[1, 2], [3, 4]] and [[5, 6], [7, 8]] is:" with the options [[19, 22], [43, 50]], [[21, 24], [45, 50]], [[17, 20], [41, 50]] and [[19, 22], [43, 48]]. Step 1 - Top left: row 1 of the left matrix against column 1 of the right, giving 1(5) + 2(7) = 5 + 14 = 19. Step 2 - Top right: 1(6) + 2(8) = 6 + 16 = 22. Bottom left: 3(5) + 4(7) = 15 + 28 = 43. Bottom right: 3(6) + 4(8) = 18 + 32 = 50. Step 3 - Read off the matrix: [[19, 22], [43, 50]], which is the answer. Step 4 - Why the others fail: [[21, 24], [45, 50]] adds the entries instead of multiplying them, [[17, 20], [41, 50]] multiplies along the rows rather than down the columns, and [[19, 22], [43, 48]] gets the top row right and then slips on the bottom right, where 18 + 32 = 50 and not 48.\n\nAnother: Now a three by three determinant, which is the step most candidates skip. The question reads, "The determinant of the matrix [[2, 1, 3], [1, 0, 2], [4, 5, 1]] is:" and the options are 1, 2, 3 and 0. Step 1 - Expand along the first row, using the sign pattern + - +, so the determinant is 2 x first minor - 1 x second minor + 3 x third minor. Step 2 - First minor: deleting row 1 and column 1 leaves [[0, 2], [5, 1]], whose determinant is (0 x 1) - (2 x 5) = 0 - 10 = -10. Second minor: deleting row 1 and column 2 leaves [[1, 2], [4, 1]], giving (1 x 1) - (2 x 4) = 1 - 8 = -7. Third minor: deleting row 1 and column 3 leaves [[1, 0], [4, 5]], giving (1 x 5) - (0 x 4) = 5 - 0 = 5. Step 3 - Substitute: 2(-10) - 1(-7) + 3(5) = -20 + 7 + 15 = 2. Step 4 - Check the signs: writing 2(-10) + 1(-7) + 3(5) gives -20 - 7 + 15 = -12, which is not among the options, and an answer of 0 comes from dropping the middle term altogether. The determinant is 2, so the answer is 2.\n\nWatch out: Two slips cost marks here. The determinant is ad MINUS bc, never ad plus bc. And a determinant of zero means the matrix is singular, so no inverse exists; a question may hand you a singular matrix on purpose to see whether you notice.\n\nIn short: add by matching entries, multiply row by column, take a 2 by 2 determinant as ad - bc, and check any inverse by multiplying it back to the identity matrix.'
